{"alignments":[],"candidates":[{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 12","reason":"Each squared term is nonnegative, so x-3=y-4=z-5=0. Therefore x+y+z=3+4+5=12.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1997_AMC_12_Q03","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_074"],"id":"nonnegative-squares","label":"Use nonnegativity of squares","methodDirectNodeIds":{"nonnegative-squares":["math:nonnegative-sum-zero"]},"nodeIds":["math:nonnegative-sum-zero","cnm_074"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"-1, with x=2 and y=-3.","reason":"The equation becomes (x-2)^2+(y+3)^2=0. For real x and y, both squares must vanish, so x+y=2-3=-1.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":["The usual school real-number domain is required; the conclusion is not valid for unrestricted complex variables."],"questionId":"cnm_153_q5","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"id":"complete-both-squares","label":"Rewrite as a zero sum of real squares","nodeIds":["math:completing-square-expression","math:nonnegative-sum-zero"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"use-real-root-discriminant","label":"Use existence of a real root and the discriminant","methodDirectNodeIds":{"use-real-root-discriminant":["cnm_223","cnm_153","cnm_220","math:nonnegative-sum-zero"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_223","cnm_153","cnm_220","math:nonnegative-sum-zero"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_148"],"id":"solution:complete-both-squares:2d65b79f259c","label":"Rewrite as a zero sum of squares","methodDirectNodeIds":{"complete-both-squares":["math:completing-square-expression","math:nonnegative-sum-zero"]},"nodeIds":["math:completing-square-expression","math:nonnegative-sum-zero","cnm_148"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: 2","reason":"Rearranging and completing squares gives (x-5)²+(y+3)²=0. Both squares are nonnegative, so x=5 and y=-3, yielding x+y=2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2013_AMC_10B_Q11","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_148","cnm_074"],"id":"solution:complete-both-squares:2ee268746697","label":"Reveal a sum of squares","methodDirectNodeIds":{"complete-both-squares":["math:completing-square-expression","math:nonnegative-sum-zero"]},"nodeIds":["math:completing-square-expression","math:nonnegative-sum-zero","cnm_148","cnm_074"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B (2)","reason":"Rearranging and completing squares gives (x-5)²+(y+3)²=0. Since both squares are nonnegative, x=5 and y=-3, so x+y=2. Alternatively, the discriminant when treating the equation as quadratic in x is -4(y+3)², which can be nonnegative only when y=-3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2013_AMC_12B_Q06","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_148","cnm_074"],"id":"complete-squares-and-use-zero-sum","label":"Reveal a sum of squares","methodDirectNodeIds":{"complete-squares-and-use-zero-sum":["math:completing-square-expression","math:nonnegative-sum-zero"]},"nodeIds":["math:completing-square-expression","math:nonnegative-sum-zero","cnm_148","cnm_074"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A. -2","reason":"Write z=u+iv and t=|z|². Expansion gives 12t=t²+4(u+1)²+36, hence (t-6)²+4(u+1)²=0. Thus t=6 and u=-1. Since 6/z=conjugate(z), the requested value is 2u=-2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_AMC_12B_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"conjugates-and-real-squares","label":"Use conjugates to expose real squares","methodDirectNodeIds":{"conjugates-and-real-squares":["cnm_h098","cnm_h102","cnm_h103","math:completing-square-expression","math:nonnegative-sum-zero"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h098","cnm_h102","cnm_h103","math:completing-square-expression","math:nonnegative-sum-zero"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"105°","reason":"Both summands are nonnegative, so sin A = 1/2 and tan B = 1. Since A and B are acute, A = 30° and B = 45°. Thus C = 180° − 30° − 45° = 105°.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_277_q5","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_007","cnm_020"],"id":"nonnegative-summands","label":"Nonnegative terms and triangle angles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"nonnegative-summands":["math:nonnegative-sum-zero","cnm_277","cnm_115"]},"nodeIds":["math:nonnegative-sum-zero","cnm_277","cnm_115","cnm_007","cnm_020"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: no nonzero real values of k","reason":"The discriminant is k^2-4k^2=-3k^2, which is negative for every nonzero real k.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1958_AMC_12_Q10","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_148","cnm_020"],"id":"nonnegative-squares","label":"Complete the square","methodDirectNodeIds":{"nonnegative-squares":["math:completing-square-expression","math:nonnegative-sum-zero"]},"nodeIds":["math:completing-square-expression","math:nonnegative-sum-zero","cnm_148","cnm_020"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 5","reason":"Since a₀≥1, n≤2. Degree 0 gives 3; degree 1 gives 2x, x+1, x-1; degree 2 gives x². These five polynomials all have h=3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1959_AMC_12_Q48","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_032","math:nonnegative-sum-zero"],"id":"enumerate-by-degree","label":"Separate the possible degrees","methodDirectNodeIds":{"enumerate-by-degree":["cnm_h230","cnm_008"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h230","cnm_008","cnm_032","math:nonnegative-sum-zero"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 1 ordered pair: (1/2, 1)","reason":"Set x=2a and y=b. The equation becomes (1+x)(1+y)(x+y)=8xy. AM-GM bounds the factors below by 2√x, 2√y, and 2√(xy), respectively. Equality requires x=y=1, which works. Hence the unique pair is (a,b)=(1/2,1).","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2023_AMC_12A_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h018"],"id":"positive-squares","label":"Sum of nonnegative squares","methodDirectNodeIds":{"positive-squares":["cnm_162","cnm_153","math:nonnegative-sum-zero"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_162","cnm_153","cnm_h018","math:nonnegative-sum-zero"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B","reason":"Completing squares gives r²=(D²+E²-4F)/4, which must be positive for a circle with positive radius.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_h177_q4","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_148","math:nonnegative-sum-zero"],"id":"complete-squares-and-check-sign","label":"Expose the squared radius","methodDirectNodeIds":{"complete-squares-and-check-sign":["math:completing-square-expression","cnm_h176","cnm_h177"]},"nodeIds":["math:completing-square-expression","cnm_h176","cnm_h177","cnm_148","math:nonnegative-sum-zero"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: none of these; A=-60 degrees+720 degrees*k for integer k","reason":"The expression equals 2sin(A/2-60 degrees), whose minimum is -2. It occurs at A=-60 degrees+720 degrees*k, none of the listed angles.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":["No range for A is stated; the minimum is taken over unrestricted real angles measured in degrees.","No range for A is stated. The result uses unrestricted real angles measured in degrees; imposing an unstated angle interval could change the minimization problem."],"questionId":"1974_AMC_12_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_148","cnm_h060","cnm_277","cnm_h056"],"id":"nonnegative-squares-minimum","label":"Build a sum of squares","methodDirectNodeIds":{"nonnegative-squares-minimum":["cnm_h062","math:nonnegative-sum-zero"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h062","math:nonnegative-sum-zero","cnm_148","cnm_h060","cnm_277","cnm_h056"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 3","reason":"Cauchy-Schwarz gives (x²+y²+z²)²≤3(x⁴+y⁴+z⁴). Equivalently, the difference is (x²-y²)²+(y²-z²)²+(z²-x²)². Taking x=y=z=1 forces 9≤3n, so n≥3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1977_AMC_12_Q29","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_080","cnm_143"],"id":"sharp-bound-by-squares","label":"Prove the bound with squared differences","methodDirectNodeIds":{"sharp-bound-by-squares":["cnm_h019","cnm_148","cnm_036","math:nonnegative-sum-zero"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h019","cnm_148","cnm_036","math:nonnegative-sum-zero","cnm_080","cnm_143"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 9","reason":"For positive roots r,s,t, Vieta gives r+s+t=rst=a and rs+rt+st=b. Cauchy-Schwarz yields a²≥3b and b²≥3a², hence a≥3√3. Equality is attained only when r=s=t=√3, giving b=9 and polynomial (x-√3)³.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2016_AMC_12A_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_032","cnm_146","cnm_148","cnm_096"],"id":"root-identities-and-squares","label":"Bound the coefficients using the roots","methodDirectNodeIds":{"root-identities-and-squares":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","math:nonnegative-sum-zero"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","math:nonnegative-sum-zero","cnm_032","cnm_146","cnm_148","cnm_096"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, at least one of a and b is nonreal","reason":"The denominators require a≠0 and a+b≠0. Cross-multiplication gives a²+ab+b²=0. For real a,b, this is (a+b/2)²+3b²/4=0, forcing the excluded pair (0,0). Complex solutions exist with b/a=(-1±i√3)/2, allowing either one or both variables to be nonreal.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1960_AMC_12_Q39","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_156","cnm_020","cnm_h100"],"id":"solution:exclude-real-pairs-by-squares:96c397f36e76","label":"Rule out real pairs using squares","methodDirectNodeIds":{"exclude-real-pairs-by-squares":["cnm_h097","cnm_148","math:nonnegative-sum-zero","cnm_h102"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h097","cnm_148","math:nonnegative-sum-zero","cnm_h102","cnm_156","cnm_020","cnm_h100"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, √2/2","reason":"Let M,N be the midpoints of AB,CD. The equilateral faces give CM=DM=√3/2 and AB perpendicular to plane CDM. Hence PQ≥MQ≥MN. Since MN is perpendicular to CD, MN²=CM²−CN²=3/4−1/4=1/2. Equality occurs at P=M and Q=N, so the minimum is √2/2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1979_AMC_12_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_174","cnm_075","cnm_080"],"id":"spatial-coordinate-squares","label":"Make the fixed separation a coordinate","methodDirectNodeIds":{"spatial-coordinate-squares":["cnm_176","cnm_h151","cnm_h154","math:nonnegative-sum-zero"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_176","cnm_h151","cnm_h154","math:nonnegative-sum-zero","cnm_174","cnm_075","cnm_080"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D (35)","reason":"The equation factors as (m+n-33)Q=0, where Q=m²+n²-mn+33m+33n+1089=[(m-n)²+(m+33)²+(n+33)²]/2. Thus Q=0 only at (-33,-33). On m+n=33, the condition mn≥0 gives the 34 pairs with m=0,1,...,33. Total: 35.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1999_AMC_12_Q30","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_014","cnm_081","cnm_146"],"id":"factor-and-use-nonnegative-squares","label":"Factor and use nonnegative squares","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factor-and-use-nonnegative-squares":["math:integer-diophantine-equations","math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_148","math:nonnegative-sum-zero"]},"nodeIds":["math:integer-diophantine-equations","math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_148","math:nonnegative-sum-zero","cnm_014","cnm_081","cnm_146"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B (92)","reason":"Set (u1,u2,u3,u4,u5)=(4-a,a-b,b-c,c-d,d), with sum 4. Since P(1)>0, a unit zero z is not 1. From (z-1)P(z)=0, obtain 4=sum uj·z^(-j). Equality in the unit-circle bound forces z^j=1 whenever uj>0. Thus positive weights are supported within {2,4}, {3}, or {5}. These yield P(1) values 8,10,12,14,16, plus 12 and 20. Their sum is 92.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2012_AMC_12B_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_146","cnm_h098","cnm_h100","cnm_029"],"id":"coefficient-differences-and-real-parts","label":"Turn ordered coefficients into nonnegative differences","methodDirectNodeIds":{"coefficient-differences-and-real-parts":["math:roots-of-unity","math:integer-diophantine-equations","math:nonnegative-sum-zero"]},"nodeIds":["math:roots-of-unity","math:integer-diophantine-equations","math:nonnegative-sum-zero","cnm_146","cnm_h098","cnm_h100","cnm_029"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: 4(36+√113)","reason":"The area is at most (24×32+32×28+28×45+45×24)/2 = 2002. Since equality holds, all four adjacent radial pairs are perpendicular. The side lengths are 40, 4√113, 53, and 51, giving perimeter 144+4√113 = 4(36+√113).","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2002_AMC_12B_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_064","cnm_075","cnm_171"],"id":"solution:radial-triangle-area-bounds:ea190b6e89bd","label":"Bound the four triangle areas","methodDirectNodeIds":{"radial-triangle-area-bounds":["cnm_p150","cnm_p148","cnm_065","math:nonnegative-sum-zero","cnm_174"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p150","cnm_p148","cnm_065","math:nonnegative-sum-zero","cnm_174","cnm_064","cnm_075","cnm_171"]}]}],"layer":"ailleo","meaning":"Reviewed content-bound concept practice; no claim of complete source-standard coverage or learner mastery.","nextOffset":null,"nodeId":"math:nonnegative-sum-zero","status":"mapped","total":18}
